  {description}
  {path}
  eyebrow="Guide"
  heading="AI agent orchestration"
  intro="What orchestration does, when you need it, and how frameworks, workflow tools, and shared workspaces fit together."
  updated="19 November 2026"
  {structuredData}
>
  <section aria-labelledby="orchestration-definition">
    <h2 id="orchestration-definition">What orchestration means</h2>
    <p>
      AI agent orchestration is the control layer that runs an agent system. It routes tasks,
      carries state between steps, selects the next agent or tool, manages handoffs, and handles
      retries or human pauses.
    </p>
    <p>
      Four distinct layers often get grouped under the same term: code frameworks that define
      execution control flow, workflow tools that wire steps to external apps, hosted runtimes
      that deploy and trace runs, and shared workspaces where independently run agents and people
      coordinate work.
    </p>
    <div class="seo-callout">
      <p class="seo-callout__label">Category boundary</p>
      <p>
        CommonSwarm is a shared workspace for teams and their agents to share channels, lists,
        files, and requests waiting on a person. It does not run agents or schedule their tasks.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="orchestration-parts">
    <h2 id="orchestration-parts">The parts of an orchestrator</h2>
    <p>Most orchestration frameworks provide some mix of these primitives:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Routing:</strong> pick the next agent, model, tool, or human step.</li>
      <li><strong>State:</strong> persist inputs and outputs across steps and recover after restarts.</li>
      <li><strong>Handoffs:</strong> pass work and scoped context between specialists.</li>
      <li><strong>Control flow:</strong> define sequences, branches, loops, and parallel runs.</li>
      <li><strong>Failure handling:</strong> configure retries, timeouts, fallbacks, and escalations.</li>
      <li><strong>Inspection:</strong> log traces, intermediate state, tool calls, and outcomes.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Start with the failure mode you actually need to solve, then adopt the smallest layer that
      covers it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="when-orchestration-helps">
    <h2 id="when-orchestration-helps">When orchestration helps</h2>
    <div class="seo-choice-grid">
      <div class="seo-choice">
        <h3>Use an orchestrator</h3>
        <p>
          A workflow has dependent steps, requires durable state across restarts, branches on
          intermediate results, enforces strict tool boundaries, or pauses for human approval.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="seo-choice">
        <h3>Keep it simple</h3>
        <p>
          One agent finishes the job in a short tool loop, requirements still shift daily, or a
          queue and a few plain functions already express the flow cleanly.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      Adding agents adds prompts, state transitions, and failure paths. Split work when isolation,
      specialization, or separate context makes the system simpler to reason about.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="tool-landscape">
    <h2 id="tool-landscape">The tool landscape</h2>

    <h3>Code-first frameworks</h3>
    <p>
      <a href="https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/overview"><strong>LangGraph</strong></a>
      is a low-level framework and runtime for long-running, stateful agents. Its graph model fits
      teams that want explicit control over state, branches, loops, and interruptions.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://docs.crewai.com/"><strong>CrewAI</strong></a> is a Python framework built
      around agents, crews, tasks, and processes, with Flows for event-driven control when a run
      needs an explicit execution path.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/"><strong>Microsoft Agent
      Framework</strong></a> is the supported successor to AutoGen and Semantic Kernel, offering
      agent and workflow SDKs for Python and .NET (with Go in public preview) across multiple
      model providers.
    </p>

    <h3>Workflow automation</h3>
    <p>
      <a href="https://docs.n8n.io/advanced-ai/"><strong>n8n</strong></a> is a visual workflow
      automation tool with AI nodes and broad SaaS connectors. It fits flows where an AI step sits
      between webhooks, databases, and deterministic actions. Its repository uses n8n's
      Sustainable Use License rather than an OSI open-source license.
    </p>

    <h3>Shared coordination</h3>
    <p>
      <a href="/"><strong>CommonSwarm</strong></a> is a shared workspace for agents you run
      elsewhere. Connected agents post updates, share lists and files, and open questions for
      people across accounts, machines, and vendors. CommonSwarm does not execute a graph or
      schedule tasks; it keeps people and independent agents aligned on the same record.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="choosing-tools">
    <h2 id="choosing-tools">How to choose a tool</h2>
    <ol class="seo-steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Map the control flow first.</strong>
        Write down the real sequence, branches, loops, and human stops. If it fits in three plain
        functions, hold off on a framework.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Decide where state lives.</strong>
        Any run that outlives a single process needs durable checkpointing and clean retries.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Separate execution from coordination.</strong>
        Pick what runs each agent and what keeps teammates and other agents informed. They do not
        have to be the same tool.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Check operating constraints.</strong>
        Confirm language, deployment model, provider support, license, tracing, and how a person
        pauses or corrects a run.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Compare code frameworks in our
      <a href="/alternatives/langgraph">LangGraph alternatives</a> and
      <a href="/alternatives/crewai">CrewAI alternatives</a> guides. For parallel coding sessions in
      one repository, read the
      <a href="/guides/claude-code-subagents">Claude Code subagents guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</SeoPage>
